    <h1 class="page-title">Pipeline Parameters (String, Boolean, Choice)</h1>

    <div class="note-card">
      
        <h2>Why Parameters in a Pipeline?</h2>
        <p>Instead of manually selecting parameters through the GUI every time, we can define them directly in the pipeline code using a <code>parameters</code> block placed right after <code>agent</code>.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>On the <strong>first build</strong>, Jenkins simply runs the code and registers the parameters.</li>
            <li>After the first build, go to Configure and confirm the parameters were picked up, then Save.</li>
            <li>From the <strong>second build onward</strong>, use <strong>"Build with Parameters"</strong> to actually provide values and see the parameter-driven output.</li>
            <li>You typically need to build twice to see the real output the first time.</li>
            <li>When using parameters, you don't need a separate <code>environment</code> block.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>String Parameter</h2>
        <pre><code>pipeline {
    agent any
    parameters {
        string(name: "person", defaultValue: "Sandeep", description: "")
    }
    stages {
        stage('Hello') {
            steps {
                sh 'echo "hi my name is $person"'
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

        <h2>Boolean Parameter</h2>
        <pre><code>pipeline {
    agent any
    parameters {
        booleanParam(name: "db", defaultValue: true, description: "")
    }
    stages {
        stage('Hello') {
            steps {
                sh 'echo "hi this is boolean parameters"'
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
        <p>If <code>defaultValue</code> is <code>true</code>, the checkbox appears checked (enabled) on the Build with Parameters screen; <code>false</code> leaves it unchecked.</p>

        <h2>Choice Parameter</h2>
        <pre><code>pipeline {
    agent any
    parameters {
        choice(name: "file", choices: ["sandeep", "chikkala", "sandy", "bablu"], description: "")
    }
    stages {
        stage('Choice Parameter') {
            steps {
                sh 'echo "touch $file"'
            }
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
        <p>On "Build with Parameters" you get a dropdown to pick one of the listed choices, which then feeds into the shell command.</p>
        
